{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"Verified in SCOPe release 2.08: ferritins are family a.25.1.1 Ferritin (SUNID 47241), ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins are family a.25.1.2 Ribonucleotide reductase-like (SUNID 47253), and manganese catalases are family a.25.1.3 Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (SUNID 100951; protein-level node 47263). All three sit in superfamily a.25.1 Ferritin-like (SUNID 47240), fold a.25 Ferritin-like, class a (all alpha proteins). The superfamily homology record has homology_implied_by_level true with homology_scope sf, so SCOPe makes a positive superfamily-level homology assertion, which supports probable common ancestry at the superfamily level. The superfamily annotation comment reads 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'; that is a curated annotation of a shared structural feature, not a residue-level mapping. The family nodes I retrieved carry no warnings, heterogeneity, repeats, or inconsistencies.

Interpretation: the three should be read as separate families inside one homologous superfamily. SCOPe does not place them in a shared family, so the closest verified relationship is at the superfamily level, and the family split records divergence within that shared architecture. The chemical differences between them do not contradict SCOPe's placement; they are what functional divergence within a superfamily looks like. A shared superfamily supports common ancestry but not shared biochemistry, so the superfamily placement alone cannot say that any member performs any particular reaction.

Limits: the enzymatic and storage chemistries (iron storage and oxidation for ferritin, radical generation for RNR R2, and H2O2 dismutation for Mn catalase) are background knowledge. SCOPe returned no reaction, substrate, or mechanism annotation for these families, so they are not verified here and should not be inferred for any member from its placement. SCOPe gives no order of divergence and no sequence or structural basis for it. I retrieved no sequence or structure alignment between these families, and I did not run domain-level BLAST or FATCAT comparisons or check domain-level representatives beyond search hits. A name search for 'ribonucleotide reductase' returned no results, so the RNR-like placement came from the superfamily's child listing rather than from a name match. These families should not be called analogs, convergent, or nonhomologous; the verified SCOPe position is superfamily-level homology. To go further, one would need a same-release domain lineage comparison of representative ferritin, RNR-like, and Mn catalase domains, plus sequence or structure alignments and primary literature for the bimetal-site residues and the mechanistic claims.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ferritin&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese+catalase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ribonucleotide+reductase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/children?limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/1009